=== Three-leg reach-wall model + sharpened falsifier ===
  wall(n) = opcount(n) x compute_cache_factor(n) x alloc_factor(n)
  leg 1 opcount        : Theta(x)*polylog, per-Dn=2 ratio 4.0-4.4x DOWN  (S530/S531/S532b, measured to n=24)
  leg 2 compute/cache  : L1->DRAM `_mul61` bowl, one-time ~7x L3 step, per-Dn growth <=1.35x  (S529)
  leg 3 alloc/faults   : malloc/mmap + minor page-faults, charged to KERNEL stime (THIS cycle; S529 omitted it)

  live n=26 reach: cumulative stime fraction = 0.695, minflt rate 3.28e+05/s, majflt 0
  => the reach is KERNEL/ALLOCATION-BOUND: leg 3 dominates its CPU. F4 NOT triggered.

  probe (size 2^24): fresh/reused per-elem ratio = 3.94, alloc overhead = 10.70 ns/elem, fresh stime frac = 0.688
  => fresh-allocation cost is page-fault (kernel) dominated at large N; reused-buffer cost is the S529 compute bowl.

  SHARPENED FALSIFIER for the harvest cycle:
    A wall ratio n24->26 > 5.4x is CONSISTENT with Theta(x) op-count IFF the
    n=26 reach is stime-dominated (leg 3, allocation).  Check the reach's
    stime/utime (e.g. /proc or `time`), not just the wall.  Only a wall excess
    that (i) survives subtracting allocation stime AND (ii) is utime-dominated
    with super-linear per-element growth would reopen super-Theta(x).
    The allocation leg is an IMPLEMENTATION constant (removable by buffer reuse/
    out=), NOT an op-count/complexity change -- op-count is Theta(x) to n=24.
